\text{Work} = \text{Work Rate} \times \text{Time} \times \text{Number of Workers}
\] Here, the total work (number of man-days required to complete the task) remains constant. Thus, if \( N \) is the number of man-days required to complete the task in one period, we can set up the following equation: \[
15 \text{ men} \times 4 \text{ days} = 25 \text{ men} \times \text{days}
\] Hence, we can solve for the number of days (\( D \)): \[
D = \frac{15 \times 4}{25}
